<section class="band">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">For LPC Associates and licensed therapists, online across Texas</p>
    <h1>Supervision, consultation and training</h1>
    <p class="intro-wide">Reem Glasco, LPC-S, has been a Texas LPC supervisor since 2009. There
    are four ways to work with her professionally: <strong>LPC supervision</strong> at $425 a
    month for three individual hours and one group hour, <strong>EMDR consultation</strong> at
    $185 an hour with an EMDRIA Approved Consultant, <strong>ethics CEUs</strong> on secondary
    trauma, and <strong>retreats and equine training</strong> for clinicians who want to develop
    their own presence. All of it runs online except the retreats, the in-person CEU and the
    horses. Here is what each one costs, and how it runs.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Is this you?</p>
    <h2>Who tends to work well here</h2>
    <div class="cards">
      <div>
        <h3>The associate who wants more than hours</h3>
        <p>You could get cheaper supervision. What you want is someone who will tell you what
        actually happened in that session, and help you build something after licensure.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>The therapist whose EMDR keeps stalling</h3>
        <p>Preparation looks fine, then processing floods or flatlines. Usually that is a part
        of the client objecting, and it is workable once you can see it.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>The clinician running on empty</h3>
        <p>Competent, respected, quietly depleted. You teach regulation skills you no longer
        practice. That is what the retreats are for.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>The one drawn to body-based work</h3>
        <p>You have read about somatic approaches and want to practice inside one. NARM,
        Somatic Experiencing and IFS-informed EMDR are the working lens here, not electives.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Not four separate purchases</p>
  <h2>Why these belong together</h2>
  <p class="intro-wide">Most therapists assemble this from four providers who have never met
  and who know nothing about each other's work with you: a supervisor for the hours, someone
  else for EMDR consultation, a vendor for the ethics requirement, and a retreat if there is
  time and money left. Here it is one relationship, and that changes what each part can do.</p>
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h3>The EMDR consultation is included</h3>
      <p>Individual EMDR consultation is <strong>$185 an hour</strong> on its own. While you are
      supervised here it is free, so hours toward EMDRIA certification accumulate alongside your
      3,000 toward licensure instead of waiting until after them.</p>
      <h3>The ethics hours fill the half that is hard to fill</h3>
      <p>Since January 2024 at least half of your 24 continuing education hours must come from a
      provider listed in 22 TAC §681.140(f), and (f)(6) is a licensee with supervisor status.
      Reem holds it, so her CEUs count toward that half rather than the free one.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>One lens, not four curricula</h3>
      <p>NARM, Somatic Experiencing and IFS-informed EMDR are the frame for all of it: the
      supervision hour, the consultation hour, the trail and the CEU. What you learn in one is
      usable in the next, rather than a separate vocabulary to carry.</p>
      <h3>It spans the career, not the credential</h3>
      <p>Supervision ends at licensure. Certification, ethics hours and your own depletion do
      not. Staying means working with someone who already knows how you think under pressure,
      which is the part that takes years to build anywhere else.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

